Q: How to make a button that switches between two colours? A: <p>It might be easier for you to use <code>onfocus</code> and <code>onblur</code>. Try out the below code and see if that's the desired result:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var login = document.querySelector('#login');
var signup = document.querySelector('#signup');



login.onfocus = function() {
  login.style.backgroundColor = "#a8661f";
}
login.onblur = function() {
  login.style.backgroundColor = "#d18029";
}

signup.onfocus = function() {
  signup.style.backgroundColor = "#a8661f";
}
signup.onblur = function() {
  signup.style.backgroundColor = "#d18029";
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>* {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

div {
  text-align: center;
  width: 80%;
  margin: 50px auto;
}

button {
  padding: 10px 20px;
  cursor: pointer;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 5px;
  margin: 0 5px;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;button id="login"&gt;Login&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;button id="signup"&gt;Sign up&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: How to access Resources (.resx) text from View and Models A: <p>Folloing <a href="http://afana.me/post/aspnet-mvc-internationalization.aspx" rel="nofollow">this</a> view of point I add a separate project just for resource files and add the reference to web application project.</p>

<p>Then I'll use not only on my dataannotations, also works on my controllers and views without use imports or using statement:</p>

<pre><code>@section scripts {
   &lt;script type="text/javascript"&gt;

      $(function () {
         $("#frm").data("CorregirError", "@Resources.CorregirError");
      });

   &lt;/script&gt;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Typescript build options on Console app in ASP.NET Web Application A: <p>I found solution: in <code>ProjectName.csproj</code> just add</p>

<pre><code>&lt;PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'"&gt;
   &lt;TypeScriptModuleKind&gt;amd&lt;/TypeScriptModuleKind&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: CSS counter-reset inside sibling div doesn&#39;t work A: <p>If you want two independent list counters you could use two independent list counters. Like so:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.list-ol1 {
  counter-reset: itm1;
  list-style-type: none;
}

.list-ol1 .item {
  counter-increment: itm1;
}

.list-ol1 .item::before {
  content: counters(itm1, ".") ". ";
}

.list-ol2 {
  counter-reset: itm2;
  list-style-type: none;
}

.list-ol2 .item {
  counter-increment: itm2;
}

.list-ol2 .item::before {
  content: counters(itm2, ".") ". ";
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>List 1
&lt;ol class="list-ol1"&gt;
  &lt;li class="item"&gt;Item 1&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li class="item"&gt;Item 2&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li class="item"&gt;Item 3 
    &lt;ol class="list-ol1"&gt;
      &lt;li class="item"&gt;item 3.1&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;/ol&gt;
  &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
  List 2
  &lt;ol class="list-ol2"&gt;
    &lt;li class="item"&gt;Item 1&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li class="item"&gt;Item 2
      &lt;ol class="list-ol2"&gt;
        &lt;li class="item"&gt;item 2.1&lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;/ol&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>You only need a separate class, or id, for the counter properties. All other CSS properties of the lists could be placed in a common class.</p>

Q: Why does a flex item overflow its container when using min-width: 100%? A: <p>So, <code>.container</code> has some width determined by the screen and the CSS rule that says it should be <code>100%</code>. It has a <code>display: flex</code> rule which defines its display property and has two children, <code>.sidebar</code> and <code>content</code>. The width of <code>.sidebar</code> is <code>220px</code> and since you defined <code>min-width: 100%</code> for <code>.content</code>, it follows that the content's width is at least as wide as its parent, which means that it will be at least 220px more than it should. So, the solution is to define the exact width of <code>.content</code> to be <code>calc(100% - 220px)</code>, which will be the remainder width except for sidebar as you intended:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.container {
  background-color: #ffeeee;
  display: flex;
  width: 100%;
}

.sidebar {
  background-color: #eeffee;
  width: 220px;
  flex-shrink: 0;
}

.content {
  width: calc(100% - 220px);
  padding: 20px;
  overflow-wrap: break-word;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="container"&gt;
  &lt;div class="sidebar"&gt;Sidebar&lt;/div&gt;

  &lt;div class="content"&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      This is a long piece of text that should wrap inside the content
      column without creating horizontal page overflow.
    &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p><code>min-width</code> is set to define the width in comparison to which your size cannot be smaller. Yet, the issue is that the value was wrong and therefore your code forced the width to be at least 220px larger than you indended.</p>
